## Capability and resource-cleanup audit

- No real Docker/container launch occurred. Every command/stdio/process assertion used injected fake processes.
- No OpenRouter/provider/pricing call, network access, replay/parity capture, cloud task, upload, push, or merge occurred.
- The production runner requires a revalidated `DockerCapability`; the fake path is available only when a non-default injected process factory is supplied.
- Docker argv is a list with no shell and includes the exact network, filesystem, user, capability, PID, memory, CPU, tmpfs, mount, and Python `-B` restrictions from the brief.
- `close_fds=True`; only stdin/stdout/stderr are pipes. The engine environment is an allowlist and excludes the test credential.
- The mounted package contains only `core/__init__.py`, strategy-policy `__init__.py`, trusted contracts/worker, and the three candidate policy modules. It excludes the repository, `.git`, PIT artifacts, credentials, and unrelated source.
- Each worker uses a fresh 16-byte nonce and 32-byte HMAC key, delivered once through bootstrap; secrets are not written to the report or retained package.
- Success, method timeout, and post-launch initialization failure tests all prove process wait/termination and private package-tree removal. Session closure is idempotent. Stderr is fully drained and hashed while retention is capped at 64 KiB.
- Method timeout is capped at one second; fold timeout is fixed at 900 seconds. JSON-lines input/output is capped at 16 KiB.
- Candidate-apply failure restores the three policy files to their exact pre-attempt bytes, including cumulative-bound and cancellation/error paths.

## Self-review

- Preserved the sole contract `PatchBounds` type and legacy 4/25/400/256-KiB defaults; PIT hard ceilings are 3/12/200/64-KiB and supplied canary bounds may only be tighter.
- Both incremental and fresh Git-derived cumulative diffs are parsed and validated under the same supplied bounds.
- Candidate identity contains authenticated commit/interface/constraint/discovery inputs, cumulative digest, exact final file hashes, controller-derived paths/symbols, and a canonical identity digest. Caller-authored cumulative text is never an identity input.
- Author paths/symbols must equal controller-derived identity exactly and otherwise fail with `author_manifest_mismatch`.
- AST validation is closed and compile-backed; it rejects ambient capabilities, mutation/state, reflection/dynamic execution, async/generator constructs, unknown public symbols, and non-allowlisted imports.
- Provider context is complete-or-reject: exact three files, exact cumulative diff, feedback caps/history caps, no truncation, and distinct `next_context_oversize` versus `context_budget_exhausted` failures.
- Protocol parsing is closed, duplicate-aware, canonical, typed, sequenced, chained, request/method bound, digest checked, and HMAC authenticated.
- Final review fixed four issues found during implementation: cumulative-failure rollback, compile-only syntax errors, launched-but-unowned process cleanup, and precise no-op classification.

## Concerns / follow-up notes

- The project currently emits `PytestConfigWarning: Unknown config option: cache_dir`; it is pre-existing and did not affect exit status.
- Git reports LF-to-CRLF normalization notices on Windows for four existing tracked files; `git diff --check` is clean.
- Per the Task 5 safety boundary, real Docker integration and replay/parity validation were intentionally not run here and remain for their planned later tasks.

